<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahapana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st–2nd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nahapana was the most powerful ruler of the Kshaharata line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a>, who held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and much of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> in the late 1st or early 2nd century CE. Inscriptions of his son-in-law Ushavadata in the caves at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> and Karla record his rule and generous gifts to Buddhist monks and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>. He was defeated by the Satavahana king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a>, who restruck thousands of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins found in the Jogaltembi hoard.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahapana.jpg" alt="Nahapana" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uploadalt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_coin_of_Nahapana_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nahapana's name appears on his coins in Kharosthi, Brahmi and Greek forms, all derived from a Saka name meaning protector of the clan, and one of his coins names him as the son of Bhumaka. His dates are uncertain. A group of his inscriptions is dated to the years 41 to 46 of an unnamed era; scholars who take it to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saka-era">Shaka era</a>, beginning in 78 CE, place his reign in 119-124 CE, while others propose 24-70 CE (Krishna Chandra Sagar) or about 66-71 CE (R. C. C. Fynes).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Modern scholars identify him with Nambanus, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periplus-of-the-erythraean-sea">Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</a> names as ruler of the coast around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharuch">Barygaza</a>, a fertile country of wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> with its metropolis at Minnagara. He established the Kshatrapa coinage in a style derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> coins, with the ruler's profile and a Greek legend on one side and a thunderbolt and arrow with Brahmi and Kharosthi legends on the other.</p><p>His son-in-law Ushavadata, husband of his daughter Dakshamitra, built rest-houses, gardens and tanks at Bharukachchha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandsaur">Dashapura</a>, Govardhana and Shorparaga, campaigned north on his orders to rescue the Uttamabhadras from the Malavas, and gave a cave near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nasik</a> to Buddhist monks. An inscription at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junnar">Junnar</a> names Ayama as his minister.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>An inscription in Nasik Cave No. 3 says that the Khakharata race was rooted out, which may mean that his heirs were killed. James Burgess thought that as much as a century might separate Nahapana from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautamiputra-satakarni">Gautamiputra Satakarni</a>, but most historians now hold that they were contemporaries. A Jain tradition, recorded in the Śrutavatāra of Bibudha Sridhara, says that he abdicated and became the monk Bhutabali, one of the two authors of the Ṣaṭkhaṇḍāgama, the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/digambara">Digambara</a> sacred text.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nahapana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yavanesvara translated the Yavanajataka, India's first Sanskrit work on horoscopy, at <b>Nahapana</b>'s court; the Kunindas, Satavahanas and others copied Indo-Greek coinage, a legacy traceable to the Guptas in the 4th century, and the Indo-Scythians who followed kept Greek mints, legends and gods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the late first and early second centuries CE the Kshaharata ruler <b>Nahapana</b> occupied much of the western Deccan, including Nashik and the ports of the Konkan, as his family's inscriptions in the local caves show.</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first line, the Kshaharatas, reached its height under <b>Nahapana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a></li></ul></section>
